\par\par \b \uc2  Israel's Leon Expects  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Market To Stabilize \b0\par\par\uc2 312 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 1998\par 13:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM (Dow Jones)--Moshe Leon, director-general of the Israeli prime minister's office, views the shekel's (ILS) strengthening Friday as a sign that calm will return to the local foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week. \par \par \uc2  Leon told reporters at a press conference in Tel Aviv that the "foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is heading towards stability. I expect to see a continuation of what happened Friday, that the calm will continue on Tuesday and we'll see dollar sales in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2  The shekel gained 1.2% to the dollar at its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixing Friday, to ILS4.2600 from ILS4.3110 on Thursday. The market will be closed Monday for a Jewish holiday. \par \par \uc2  Leon's remarks followed a week of tumultuous trading on the Israeli foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, in which the shekel broke through the ILS4.000 level to the dollar for the first time ever and sparked fears the country was heading for a steep inflationary spiral. \par \par \uc2  Many analysts expect to see the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading if the shekel's descent continues this week, although economic policymakers have pledged to stay out of the market for now. \par \par \uc2  The sharp and speedy depreciation - 10.8% since September alone - was touched off by the outflow of foreign investor capital and by Israeli customers seeking to cover their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt. Most of the trading was interbank, with few business sector customers in the market due to the abbreviated work week of the Succoth holiday. \par \par \uc2  Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers and analysts said the absence of many business sector customers exacerbated the shekel's fall, since the low liquidity of trading deepened the impact of individual transactions. Individual customers are also more likely to sell dollars at such high levels against the shekel, they said. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 11-10-98 \par \par \uc2  1152GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917duab002zf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
