\par\par \b \uc2  Analysts say Bank of Israel must defend shekel. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Rosenberg \par \uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 1998\par 14:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Oct 8 (Reuters) - The tumbling shekel does not signal market concerns about the Israeli economy, but with the dollar now well above 4.3 shekels the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a free fall, analysts said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 10 percent against the dollar since last Friday, when it came under selling pressure mainly from foreign banks reacting to global fears over emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the shekel was fixed 5.0 percent lower on the dollar at 4.3110. \par \par \uc2  "We're not seeing a speculative attack on the shekel per se. Neither foreigners nor the Israeli private sector has lost confidence in the fundamentals," said Jonathan Katz, chief economist at Solid Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  But Katz and other analysts warned that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not quickly send the market a message that it would not tolerate further sharp losses for the shekel, the floodgates could open. \par \par \uc2  The Israeli economy has little exposure to hot foreign capital, but domestic borrowers have as much as 83.3 billion shekels ($19.3 billion) in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated loans outstanding, most of it through Israeli banks. \par \par \uc2  "I don't see the situation as catastrophic but it could become so if companies decide all at once to pay back loans. That would show panic," said Yehuda Ben-Assayag, managing director of Koor Investment House. \par \par \uc2  Israelis got hooked on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowing in the mid-1990s when high domestic interest rates and a stable shekel encouraged interest rate arbitrage. \par \par \uc2  The shekel has become more volatile, especially since the government abandoned most of its remaining \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls last April. The shekel has lost 18 percent on the dollar so far this year, but most borrowers have held on to their loans. \par \par \uc2  By \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be signalling that it will not let the exchange rate run wild. And, with foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of nearly $22 billion, the bank has the ammunition to back up its claim. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the Bank of Israel would almost certainly raise interest rates, widening what has been a narrowing differential with rates abroad. Most said the rate would have to rise between one and 1.5 percentage point by the end of the year from a current base rate of 9.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  But they said that was less important than direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for defending the shekel. \par \par \uc2  "Because of the panic, no one is interested in the interest rate gap. But seeing the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have a big effect on Israeli players (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) at least," said Adam Reuter, of the Finanical Immunities consulting firm. \par \par \uc2  "Israelis are much, much bigger in the market than foreigners," he said. \par \par \uc2  Reuter said most borrowers had little reason to repay loans and that the banks would do little to force them to repay. \par \par \uc2  "The banks pressured borrowers long ago to buy hedging instruments," he said. "Some are naked and might be in trouble, but the banks won't press them too far for fear that they'll go squash." \par \par \uc2  Reuter said Israeli banks would probably take a hit on their earnings, but he noted that they were financially sound and had little or no exposure to imploding emerging markets abroad. \par \par \uc2  But Ben Assayag was less sanguine. Builders and other companies will see their \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated debt load balloon, he warned. \par \par \uc2  Banking sources said the building and property sector alone had about $3.8 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  credit outstanding, and the industry is already suffering from a prolonged slump. \par \par \uc2  He said that even export companies are not immune, because many are now facing softer export markets, especially in Israel's key high technology sector. \par \par \uc2  "If the shekel reaches 4.5 to the dollar, companies will begin to fail and this will reverberate on the banks," Ben Assayag said. \par \par \uc2  ($1 = 4.3110 shekels) \par \par \uc2  ((Jerusalem newsroom, +972 2 537-0502, jerusalem.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113dua801hp0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
