\par\par \b \uc2  Israeli shekel breaches 4.3 to the dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 216 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 1998\par 12:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV, Oct 8 (Reuters) - The Israeli shekel breached the 4.3 barrier against the dollar just after noon on Thursday, a fall that could prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 1015 GMT, the shekel was trading at 4.3180 against the dollar. The shekel has been steadily losing ground to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the end of last week and breached the 4.0 barrier on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, many analysts were saying that they expected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar rose above 4.3 shekels. But dealers said they had not yet seen any indications of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There doesn't appear to be any sign," said a dealer at Israel Discount Bank, adding that she did not know why the dollar took a big jump around noon time. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel was due to set the official representative rate shortly, although trading continues afterward. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the shekel was under pressure from interbank trading and from Israeli borrowers seeking to repay foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -linked loans early. A dealer at Israel Discount Bank said the public was not buying dollars. \par \par \uc2  ((Tel Aviv newsroom, +972 3 537-2211 x238, telaviv.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113dua801hjy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
