\par\par \b \uc2  Israel shekel at 4.22 to the dollar in early trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 213 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 1998\par 10:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV, Oct 8 (Reuters) - The Israeli shekel slid in early trading against the dollar on Thursday amid heavy dollar-buying by foreign banks, deaers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0750 GMT, the shekel was trading at 4.2148 to the dollar, versus a Wednesday fix of 4.0960. The shekel has been steadily losing ground to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the end of last week and breached the 4.0 barrier on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not yet \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, but one said he thought the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step in if the rate reached 4.3. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at Mercantile Discount Bank said the dollar would meet resistance at 4.3 shekels but said it was not a strong barrier. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the shekel was under pressure from inter-bank trading and from Israeli borrowers seeking to repay foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -linked loans early. A dealer at Israel Discount Bank said the public was not buying dollars. \par \par \uc2  Against the basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the shekel was trading 4.6545 at 0755 GMT, against a Wednesday fix of 4.5084. \par \par \uc2  ((Tel Aviv newsroom, +972 3 537-2211 x238, telaviv.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113dua801hdj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
