\par\par \b \uc2  Israeli Official/Shekel -3: Now Close To Midpoint \b0\par\par\uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 1998\par 07:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Zilberfarb said the government is revising upward its earlier forecast for inflation this year because of the shekel's steep decline. On Dec. 31, 1997, the dollar was trading at ILS3.5360, down from ILS3.2510 a year before. \par \par \uc2  "Until two months ago we were very optimistic that we were going to finish this year (with an inflation rate) between 3% and 4%, the target we set for 2001. But with this change in the exchange rate, we'll probably end up in the area of 5%, which is still pretty good considering we were in double digits a couple of years ago," he said. \par \par \uc2  Zilberfarb said the government is optimistic that inflation will ebb to about 4% next year. \par \par \uc2  The shekel is allowed to trade 15% above and below a gradually depreciating midpoint, and earlier this year the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars and sell shekels when the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to its ceiling. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this time to slow the shekel's fall because the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still close to its midpoint, he noted. \par \par \uc2  "(The dollar was) at the bottom of the band and what's happened is that over the last couple of months we've come close to the middle," Zilberfarb said. \par \par \uc2  "Most of the shift has taken place in the last few months and has a lot to do with what's happening internationally. It's more a sense of uncertainty than of pessimism," he said. \par \par \uc2  The shekel's prolonged strength - until it weakened abruptly in the summer - had prompted private sector investors to take out foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated loans in order to profit from lower interest rates. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened when these investors became nervous about the international situation. \par \par \uc2  That's possibly another beneficial side-effect of the shekel's weakness: that it has made private-sector investors more aware of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, and made them more cautious about their foreign exposure. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Now they realize (the shekel) can go up and down," he observed,\par adding, "People will be more aware that wherever you go you have to\par calculate your risks very, very carefully."\par -By David Pearson; (202) 789 4193, e-mail:dpearson@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 08-10-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0502GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010920dua8004wc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
