\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  Authorities Won't  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Market - Reports \b0\par\par\uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 1998\par 11:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM (Dow Jones)--Israeli economic officials pledged not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets despite fears that the dollar's rise beyond the four-shekel (ILS) level may lead to an even sharper depreciation and higher inflation, Israeli newspapers reported Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Yaakov Neeman and Bank of Israel Governor Jacob Frenkel spoke to Israeli reporters Tuesday while attending a meeting of the International Monetary Fund in Washington. \par \par \uc2  "The (Israeli) market is working very well and proving itself. We cannot \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trade and defend exchange rates that aren't in line with market forces," Neeman said, according to the reports. \par \par \uc2  The shekel weakened to an all-time low Tuesday of ILS4.0110 to the dollar, and analysts expect to see the depreciation continue on the back of global economic troubles. At 0928 GMT, the shekel was trading at about ILS4.0700 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In addition, the breach of such a psychologically significant exchange level could feed a stronger spiral of decline for the shekel, as it touches off fears of higher inflation and capital flight. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could try to curb the depreciation by either selling dollars on the market or raising interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel played down the possibility of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , last seen at the beginning of 1998 when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars to weaken an overly strong shekel. \par \par \uc2  "The depreciation so far isn't a 'big deal,' not from an Israeli perspective and not from a world perspective," Frenkel said, according to the reports. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel noted that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is committed only to defend the upper and lower limits of the shekel's trading band, the reports said. The shekel is allowed to trade 15% above and below a gradually depreciating midpoint. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel estimated that the shekel has room to weaken some 20% before it hits its nether limit, the reports said. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 07-10-98 \par \par \uc2  0948GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010920dua70043w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
