\par\par \b \uc2  Israel interest rates seen unchanged for October. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Rosenberg \par \uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1998\par 15:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Sept 24 (Reuters) - Israeli interest rates are expected to stay unchanged for October while the Bank of Israel awaits evidence of the full inflationary impact of the shekel's recent depreciation, economists said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "If I were (Governor Jacob) Frenkel, I'd say 'Let's wait. Maybe the exchange rate will come down again and inflation won't be that high,'" said Micha Goldberg, head of research at Nessuah Zannex Securities. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will announce its October monetary programme, including the key monetary tender rate, on Sunday at 1430 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates have been coming down all this year, culminating in a surprise 1.5 percentage point cut at the start of August that put the base rate at its current level of 9.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  But economists said the Bank of Israel might have overshot on that last cut. The reduction, coupled with turbulence in world financial markets, caused the shekel to fall about five percent against the dollar, rekindling inflation. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the question was how much inflation and how long the upsurge would last. \par \par \uc2  The August consumer price index (CPI) was pushed higher by the depreciation, a trend which Baruch Snir, chief economist at the Federation of Israeli Chambers of Commerce, said would continue into September. But he said inflation would moderate in the last quarter of the year. \par \par \uc2  Full-year 1998 inflation would reach 5.0 percent, up from 4.4 percent forecast before the depreciation began, Snir estimated. \par \par \uc2  Goldberg said the critical factor in the future direction of inflation was the exchange rate, currently 3.84 shekels to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Expectations are that over the short term we'll be at 3.85 and then it will decline to 3.80. This will lead to short-term inflationary pressures," he said. \par \par \uc2  As a result, Goldberg said, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably raise rates by 0.2-0.3 of a percentage point at the end of October. \par \par \uc2  But Eran Sela, economist at Israel Discount Bank, estimated there was a 50 percent chance that Frenkel would not wait that long and would raise rates by half a percentage point as soon as Sunday. \par \par \uc2  "Inflationary expectations are very high at about 5.5 percent, which is way above the government's (4.0 percent) target for 1999. Also money supply has been rising too quickly," he said. \par \par \uc2  The bond market's inflationary expectations are a key benchmark for the Bank of Israel, but Snir said the markets were likely to revise down their inflationary outlook after the September CPI is published on October 15. \par \par \uc2  But he warned that in coming weeks there was a 30 percent chance that the dollar would lurch higher against the shekel, which would cause the bank to boost rates sharply before the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  "If we see a strong depreciation, with the shekel going to 3.95-4.00, I see one of two scenarios - either the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or it will raise interest rates by one percentage point," Snir said. \par \par \uc2  On balance, however, he said inflation could be contained even with a moderate weakening of the shekel because domestic demand remained weak and world commodity prices were falling. \par \par \uc2  As a result, Snir said, inflation in 1999 should fall to 4.0 percent, with the shekel losing about five percent against the dollar over the course of the year. That, he added, would enable interest rates to fall about one percentage point over the course of the year. \par \par \uc2  ($1 = 3.84 shekels) \par \par \uc2     ((Jerusalem newsroom, +972 2 537-0502, \par \par \uc2   jerusalem.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9o01u1s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
