\par\par \b \uc2  Israel's 1Q Short-Term External Debt Doubles At $8.45 Bln \b0\par\par\uc2 254 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 1998\par 12:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM (Dow Jones)--Israel's net short-term external debt totaled $8.45 billion at the end of the first quarter of 1998 - double the annual average of $4.3 billion from 1990 to 1996, the Bank of Israel said Monday. \par \par \uc2  Most of the rise is due to an increase in the private sector's short-term external debt, which accounts for some 80% of the economy's short-term external debt, the bank said. \par \par \uc2  Many businesses borrow abroad and convert the money into shekels to take advantage of the difference between interest rates overseas and the high interest rates due to the Bank of Israel's tight monetary policy over the past three years. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said that since the beginning of the decade, its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as a proportion of the government's overall external debt have grown to 78% at the end of the first quarter of 1998 from 38% in 1990. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves grew to $21.34 billion at the end of March 1998 from $6.795 billion at the end of 1994, in large part because the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in late 1996 and 1997 to defend the shekel's trading band. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel bought more than $7 billion in the first half of 1997 alone to weaken the shekel and keep it within its trading band. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 29-06-98 \par \par \uc2  1022GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6t05io9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\