\par\par \b \uc2  Foreign capital imports to Israel up in 1997. \b0\par\par\uc2 385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV, Feb 17 (Reuters) - Foreign investors put $3.7 billion into the Israeli economy in 1997, an increase of $1 billion over 1996, the Bank of Israel said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  About half of the increase was the result of direct investments while the other half was from financial investments. \par \par \uc2  Capital inflows by foreigners in the second half totalled $2.2 billion, up from $1.5 billion in the first half. \par \par \uc2  The Israeli private sector imported $4.8 billion in 1997, against $2.4 billion in 1996. But the imports were concentrated in the first half, when they reached $5.4 billion. \par \par \uc2  In the second half Israelis exported $570 million, marking a turnaround from a huge flow of capital imports in the previous two and a half years. \par \par \uc2  "Israeli residents effectively stopped taking credit in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the banking system in the second half of 1997 while increasing their deposits in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this period by $1.3 billion," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  Israelis borrowed $229 million in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Israeli banks in the second half against $4.2 billion in the first half. \par \par \uc2  The inflow of capital by Israeli companies attracted to lower interest rates overseas had put upward pressure on the Israeli shekel. \par \par \uc2  Because of that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in the local market to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating outside its trading band. \par \par \uc2  Israeli foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms in June and the Southeast Asian crisis increased the exchange rate risk associated with taking foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans. At the same time, the spread between domestic and foreign interest rates has narrowed. \par \par \uc2  Foreign financial investments in 1997 rose to $2.1 billion from $1.4 billion in 1996. Foreign investment in the Tel Aviv Stock Exchange more than doubled to $720 million from $335 million in 1996, though most of the increase was in the first half of 1997. \par \par \uc2  Direct investment by foreigners in 1997 rose to $1.8 billion from $1.3 billion in 1996. Much of this was the result of privatisation, such as the sale of a controlling stake in Bank Hapoalim. ((Tova Cohen, Tel Aviv newsroom, +972 3 537-2211 x238, telaviv.newsroom@reuters.com)) TCC \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du2h02nci\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
