\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Israel head offers fix for unemployment. \b0\par\par\uc2 508 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Jan 27 (Reuters) - Bank of Israel Governor Jacob Frenkel said on Tuesday that Israel should spend more on infrastructure projects, especially in transport, as a short-term solution to the country's growing unemployment. \par \par \uc2  In a statement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  quoted Frenkel as saying that to solve joblessness over the longer term, the government should be investing in "human infrastructure" and job re-training. \par \par \uc2  "The real and long-term solution to unemployment requires a focus on human infrastructure and professional re-training on all levels, and for the short term, investment in infrastructure, especially transport infrastructure," the bank said in a summary of Frenkel's remarks. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel, who was reviewing developments in the Israeli economy during 1997 before the parliamentary finance committee, has come under growing criticism for Israel's rising unemployment. \par \par \uc2  Critics say the Bank of Israel's restrictive monetary policy has been one of the chief factors in the country's economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2  "If the Israeli government and the governor continue with this policy, whose sole aim is to reduce inflation at the cost of sharply rising unemployment and slowing investment, by the end of 1998 we'll end with 9-10 percent unemployment," Abraham Shohet, finance minister in the previous Labour government, told Frenkel. \par \par \uc2  Israel's jobless rate reached 8.1 percent in the third quarter, the latest for which figures are available, versus 6.6 percent a year earlier. Gross domestic product grew a preliminary 2.1 percent last year, its slowest pace of the decade. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the Bank of Israel said it was lowering its key rate for February by 0.5 percentage point to 12.9 percent, the first cut since the end of August. \par \par \uc2  However, inflation has been falling in recent months. As a result, economists estimate the real base rate will be about 5.5 percent after the reduction. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel said the reduction in Israeli inflation last year to 7.0 percent, its lowest in 28 years, was only an interim stage on the way to the government's stated goal of bringing Israeli inflation to European levels of the start of the next century. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel said the most important contribution to Israel's economic stability last year was the reduction in its current account deficit. He said the Asian crisis had demonstrated how critical it had been to cut the deficit. \par \par \uc2  The deficit narrowed to an estimated $3.5 billion from $5.3 billion in 1996. \par \par \uc2  He said the Asian crisis had also showed that it was impossible for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to successfully counter trends in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets over the long term. \par \par \uc2  He praised last June's decision to widen the Israeli shekel's trading band against the basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to which it is linked, saying it enabled the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end a long period of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that occurred in the first half of last year. ((Jerusalem newsroom, +972 2 537-0502, jerusalem.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1r015di\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
