\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  Shekel Unchanged Vs Dollar; No  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 219 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 1998\par 15:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM (Dow Jones)--The shekel was unchanged against the dollar in moderate trading late Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel fixed the shekel at 3.5610 to the dollar, the same as Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  The shekel traded within a range of 3.5580 to 3.5680 to the dollar, dealers said. Shortly after the 1330 GMT fixing, the shekel was trading around 3.5650 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at Israel Discount Bank said demand for the dollar picked up in the afternoon over news reports that Bank of Israel Governor Jacob Frenkel, Finance Minister Yaakov Neeman and Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu would meet Tuesday to discuss the exchange rate policy. \par \par \uc2  The meeting never took place. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  renewed its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading Dec. 29 after a six-month hiatus, buying dollars to weaken the shekel, which is grazing the ceiling of its trading band. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Israel didn't have to buy foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Tuesday because\par 'a natural corporate interest' in buying dollars pushed the shekel below\par the upper range of its band, said Neil Corney, head of the spot desk at\par Bank Hapoalim.\par -By Amy Teibel; 972-2-537-6985;ateibel@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 13-01-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1404GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1092 1849 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du1d00bth\par }\page {\par\fs