\par\par \b \uc2  Israeli CPI bonds end higher for third session. \b0\par\par\uc2 513 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV, Jan 8 (Reuters) - Inflation-linked bonds closed higher for the third consecutive day on Thursday as investors were enticed by their high yields, traders said. \par \par \uc2  * Government bonds general: +0.13 pct to 112.70 \par \par \uc2  * CPI-linked government bonds: +0.16 pct to 111.39 \par \par \uc2  * Unlinked government bonds: +0.01 pct to 116.80 \par \par \uc2  * Dollar-linked government bonds: +0.09 pct to 115.16 \par \par \uc2  * Bank of Israel short-term Makam bills: +0.03 pct to 114.35 \par \par \uc2  Volume including Makams was a moderate 120 million shekels. \par \par \uc2  "Prices of CPI-linked bonds have been recovering in the past few days because of their interesting yields," said Danny Alt of Nesuah Trading and Investments. \par \par \uc2  Yields on long bonds were around 4.3 percent while those on short bonds were around five percent. \par \par \uc2  "Provident funds got a lot of deposits at the end of the year and this liquidity is allowing them to buy inflation-linked bonds at attractive levels," Alt said. \par \par \uc2  Prices of inflation-linked bonds had taken a big hit in the past few weeks, while prices of unlinked bonds had risen, as some economists revised downward their estimates for this year's consumer price index (CPI) to seven percent or less. \par \par \uc2  Makam bonds closed slightly higher on Thursday while unlinked bonds closed stable after falling the previous two days. \par \par \uc2  "Prices of unlinked bonds closed lower in the past two days as a correction to their previous gains. But yields are still attractive discounting for inflation of about 7.5 percent," said Gidi Halpern of Meitav Investments and Securities. \par \par \uc2  Yields on one-year Makams were 12.6 percent while on other unlinked bonds they ranged between 12.25 to 12.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Basically the market is waiting to see if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lower interest rates at the end of the month," Halpern said. "The market is expecting a 0.3 percentage point cut." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has held its base rate at 13.4 percent since the end of August, but many are hoping that with the passage of the 1998 budget in parliament last Monday he will lower rates later this month. \par \par \uc2  A strong dollar worldwide and hopes for a rate cut at the end of the month lifted the prices of dollar-linked bonds on Thursday, Alt said. \par \par \uc2  "The rise of the dollar worldwide along with hopes of a rate cut in Israel makes it natural that investors turn to dollar bonds," he said. \par \par \uc2  The representative rate for the dollar was fixed at 3.5650 shekels on Thursday against 3.5640 on Wednesday as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, buying $20 million to prevent the shekel from appreciating beyond its trading band. \par \par \uc2  Note: Makams are zero-coupon unlinked certificates with durations of three months to one year. The Bank of Israel issues Makams to absorb liquidity. \par \par \uc2  ($1 = 3.56 shekels) \par \par \uc2  ((Shoshanna Solomon , Tel Aviv newsroom, +972 3 537-2211 x233, telaviv.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du18019ks\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
