\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervention \highlight\uc2  was $250 mln-governor. \b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV, Jan 8 (Reuters) - Bank of Israel Governor Jacob Frenkel said on Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had spent some $250 million \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but that this did not mean a need to alter \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has indeed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in recent days. However, \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as this do not necessitate a new debate on the Bank of Israel's exchange rate policy," Frenkel told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying dollars during most of the trading sessions since last Wednesday to prevent the shekel from appreciating beyond its trading band. The band is set against a basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It is the first time the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last June, when foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform and a sharp interest rate cut temporarily weakened the shekel. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel lauded last June's reforms, saying they had enabled the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a long stretch. \par \par \uc2  "There was no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the end of December and the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market developed enormously in the last six months," he said. \par \par \uc2  "There aren't enough reserves in the world for any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the exchange rate band where there are significant one-directional market forces for a long period. The lesson we can learn from Southeast Asia is that it was right to widen the band." \par \par \uc2  Frenkel also took issue with critics of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  liberalisation, who he said were seeking to slow the process in the face of rising Israeli unemployment and concerns that Asia's woes could spread to Israel. \par \par \uc2  "Specifically in times of slowdown, you must not strangle liberalisation. If you implement the correct policies, the financial markets will support you," he said. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel stressed that Israel's approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liberalisation was gradual. But he promised key reforms would be carried out in the first half, among them increasing the percentage of assets provident funds may invest abroad. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel and Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu have said they are aiming to make the Israeli shekel fully convertible by the country's 50th anniversary at the end of April. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel governor also sounded a critical note about fiscal policy. While he praised the Treasury for bringing the budget deficit for 1997 under target, he said he was discouraged by its components. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the Treasury reported that the government deficit for 1997 was only 2.4 percent of gross domestic product, less than the targeted 2.8 percent. However, it said, the domestic deficit was wider than expected and offset by an unexpected foreign surplus. \par \par \uc2  "It is clear there must be a much bigger weight in future debate on the content rather than simply the size of the deficit," he said. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel also expressed concern that much of the reserve for the 1998 budget has reportedly been committed. \par \par \uc2  The markets are watching Frenkel's view on the budget carefully. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel has not changed interest rates since the end of August, but many are hoping that with the passage of the 1998 budget in parliament last Monday he will lower rates later this month. \par \par \uc2  ((Tel Aviv newsroom, +972 3 537-2211, telaviv.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du18019k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
