\par\par \b \uc2  Israeli CPI bonds end higher on inflation fears. \b0\par\par\uc2 491 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV, Jan 6 (Reuters) - Israeli Inflation-linked bonds rose on Tuesday, breaking a five-week-old downward trend, on fears that a strong dollar would boost inflation, traders said. \par \par \uc2  * Government bonds general: +0.12 pct to 112.43 \par \par \uc2  * CPI-linked government bonds: +0.13 pct to 111.11 \par \par \uc2  * Unlinked government bonds: -0.05 pct to 116.86 \par \par \uc2  * Dollar-linked government bonds: +0.51 pct to 114.97 \par \par \uc2  * Bank of Israel short-term Makam bills: unchanged at 114.31 \par \par \uc2  Volume including Makams was a moderate 180 million shekels. \par \par \uc2  "The strengthening dollar against the shekel has raised fears that inflation levels will not be as low as previously expected. Traders are starting to go back to inflation-linked bonds," said Gila Shkury, investment adviser at Israel Discount Bank. \par \par \uc2  The shekel's representative rate on Tuesday was fixed lower against the dollar, after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose against the mark and yen overseas. The representative rate for the dollar was fixed at 3.5630 shekels against 3.5520 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying dollars to prevent the shekel from appreciating beyond its trading band. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began buying dollars last week, marking its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last June. \par \par \uc2  "If the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by buying dollars, it means the dollar can only go up," said Roy Meir of Simodan brokerage. \par \par \uc2  The higher dollar also lifted the prices of dollar-linked bonds, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Inflation-linked bonds had taken a big hit in the past few weeks, while unlinked bond prices had risen, as some economists said the consumer price index (CPI) could rise by less than seven percent next year. \par \par \uc2  Since December 1, prices of CPI-linked bonds have fallen sharply, with the index bottoming out at 110.97 on Monday from 113.19 on December 1. \par \par \uc2  Meir said he expected inflation-linked bonds would continue to recover in the coming days while unlinked bonds fell slightly. "Yields on unlinked bonds are not as attractive as they were," he said. \par \par \uc2  Yields on one-year Makams were 12.6 percent and those on other unlinked bonds ranged between 12.3 to 12.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  Meir said the market had not been affected by the budget's passage in parliament on Monday after the end of trading, but Shkury said he believed it paved the way for an interest rate cut in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  "There are expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lower interest rates by up to one percent point in the next quarter," Shkury said. The base rate is currently 13.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  Note: Makams are zero-coupon unlinked certificates with durations of three months to one year. The Bank of Israel issues Makams to absorb liquidity. \par \par \uc2  ($1 = 3.56 shekels) \par \par \uc2  ((Shoshanna Solomon , Tel Aviv newsroom, +972 3 537-2211 x233, telaviv.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du16015my\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
