\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  Shekel Ends Lower Vs Dlr; Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Israel Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 236 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 1997\par 15:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM (Dow Jones)--The shekel ended lower against the dollar late Wednesday, but the slight weakening didn't move the shekel from just beyond the strongest point in its trading band. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading for the second straight day Wednesday, buying between $100 and $200 million, to weaken the shekel and defend the trading band. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel fixed the shekel at 3.5360 to the dollar, a rise of 0.35% against Tuesday's close of 3.5340 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The shekel traded within a range of 3.5315 to 3.5370 to the dollar. Shortly after the 1330 GMT fixing, the shekel was trading at 3.5350. \par \par \uc2  Dollar sales have dominated the market for several days because companies needed shekels for year-end financing expenses and a dollar-shekel options expiry. \par \par \uc2  The foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market continued to feel the effect of the dollar sales on Wednesday, dealers said, which was reinforced by customers' closing short on the dollar before the New Year holiday. \par \par \uc2  Trading was light, dealers said, and the shekel remained just beyond the strongest point in its trading band throughout the day. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't release a fixed shekel-dollar rate on Jan. 1.\par -By Michele Gershberg;9722-537-6985;mgershberg@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 31-12-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1420GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1092 1849 \par \par \uc2 