\par\par \b \uc2  Israel/Shekel -2: Shekel Inching Toward Strongest Point \b0\par\par\uc2 172 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 1997\par 15:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  For the past few months the shekel has been inching its way closer to the strongest point in its trading band, strengthened by the dollar's weakening on world markets. \par \par \uc2  Customers have also returned to speculative loan-taking in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , taking advantage of the differential between Israel's 13.4% discount rate and those of other countries. \par \par \uc2  One dealer, who spoke on condition of anonymity, said chances were high that the Bank of Israel soon would have to start buying dollars to weaken the shekel and keep it within its trading band. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since it widened the shekel's trading band on June 18. \par \par \uc2  In the preceding five months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought $7 billion from the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the shekel and keep it within its trading band. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Michele Gershberg;9722-537-6985;mgershberg@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 29-12-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1417GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1092 1849 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtct09z7c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
