\par\par \b \uc2  Israel Shekel Down Late As Dollar Rises On Overseas Markets \b0\par\par\uc2 274 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 1997\par 16:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM (AP-Dow Jones)--The shekel weakened against the dollar in heavy trading late Monday following the dollar's rise on markets overseas, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel fixed the shekel at 3.4950 to the dollar, a weakening of 0.37% against Thursday's close of 3.4820 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The shekel traded within a range of 3.4850 to 3.4970 to the dollar. Shortly after the 1330 GMT fixing, the shekel was trading at 3.490. \par \par \uc2  Ilan Zadka, head trader at United Mizrahi Bank, said the shekel weakened as the dollar gained on world markets. But the shekel has been strengthening over the past four months, and Zadka said he didn't see an end to that trend. \par \par \uc2  The shekel's rise - fueled by the dollar's weakness on world markets in recent weeks and rising capital inflows to Israel - has pushed the shekel near the strongest point in its trading band. Zadka expected that within the next few weeks the Bank of Israel would be forced to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the shekel and bring it back within its trading band. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading since it changed the shekel's trading band on June 18. From early January to June 17, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought some $7 billion. \par \par \uc2  One dealer said there was talk in the market that one customer was carrying out large transactions on the market that affected its direction, but couldn't identify the customer. \par \par \uc2  (END) A