\par\par \b \uc2  Foreign capital imports to Israel jump in quarter. [CORRECTED 22:00 GMT] \b0\par\par\uc2 334 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 1997\par 19:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Oct 12 (Reuters) - Foreign investors put $1.223 billion into the Israeli economy in the third quarter, marking a sharp rise from the first half, the Bank of Israel said on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  At the same time the Israeli private sector exported $389 million in capital, marking a turnaround from a huge flow of capital imports in the previous two-and-a-half years. In the first half alone, capital imports by Israelis reached $5.340 billion. \par \par \uc2  "The main change in capital movements by Israeli residents was due to a sharp drop in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  credit taken by the private sector and a rise \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts by Israeli residents," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  Israelis borrowed $200 million in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the third quarter, compared with $4.2 billion in the first half, the Bank of Israel said. Deposits in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts grew by $856 million in the quarter after falling $180 million in the first half. \par \par \uc2  The inflow of capital by Israeli companies engaging arbitrage between high domestic interest rates and lower overseas ones had put upward pressure on the shekel. \par \par \uc2  Because of that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the first half to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating outside its trading band. \par \par \uc2  The rise in capital inflows by foreigners was mainly in financial investments, especially in overseas securities offerings by Israeli companies. Foreign investment in that category alone reached $531 million in the quarter, compared with $161 million (corrects from $616 million) in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  Inflows from direct investments grew the most slowly, reaching $357 million in the three months from $318 million in the second quarter and $435 million in the first. \par \par \uc2  Total foreign capital inflows in the first nine months reached $2.606 billion, compared with $2.690 billion for all of 1996 and $2.204 billion for all of 1995. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dtac01bj0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
