\par\par \b \uc2  Israel Shekel Up Vs Dlr, Nears Highest Point In Band \b0\par\par\uc2 255 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1997\par 15:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM (AP-Dow Jones)--The shekel was higher against the dollar in late trading Tuesday as a result of the dollar's drop on other markets. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel fixed the shekel at 3.5140 to the dollar, a strengthening of 0.14% against Monday's close of 3.5190 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The shekel traded within a range of 3.5110 to 3.5180 to the dollar. Shortly after the 1230 GMT fixing, the shekel was trading at 3.5130. \par \par \uc2  Raya Yona, head of the corporate desk at Bank Hapoalim's trading room, said the dollar's continued decline on world markets was 'the prime reason' for the shekel's rise. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted the shekel was slowly creeping toward the strongest point in its trading band, and estimated it could just be a matter of days before the Bank of Israel would be forced to re-enter the market and weaken the shekel. \par \par \uc2  'Trading volumes are so low that any one large transaction in either direction could make the difference,' noted Isaac Elias, a trader with Bank Leumi. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since it changed the shekel's trading band in mid-June. But during the first five and a half months of 1997 the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought billions of dollars in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the overly strong shekel within its trading band. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 09-09-97 \par \par \uc2  1347GMT \par \par \uc2  1092 1849 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt9906nnt\p